  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1132 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nThere is a very high chance for the high pressure system to remain \nthe dominant weather feature across the southern plains through much \nof, if not all, of next week. However just because it is the \ndominant weather feature doesn't mean it wont be dynamic with the \nstrength of the system waxing and waining and it's center moving \naround. It is that flux that will bring changing weather across the \npanhandles for next week. \n\nSunday will most likely still have the high pressure preventing \nmoisture from arriving in the panhandles keeping the weather dry.\n\nThis most likely change starting Monday as the high pressure starts \nto weaken and shift around allowing for limited moisture.\nThis reintroduces at least a low chance for rain showers and \nthunderstorms that will continue through Tuesday. \n\nFor Wednesday and the rest of the work week the high pressure is \nmore likely than not to weaken. The high pressure center is most \nlikely to shift back the desert southwest with a lesser chance for \nit to become an open ridge of high pressure. Either way this will \nallow for more moisture and small weather system to push into the \nsouthern plains. This increases the chances for rain showers and \nthunderstorms during the second half of next week.",
